H. None of these choices

The auscultatory and clinical picture is that of a fixed right ventricular outflow tract pressure ejection murmur, i.e. classic congenital pulmonary valvular stenosis, which is not listed A–G.

A harsh systolic ejection murmur maximal at the left 2nd–3rd ICS/left upper sternal border that radiates toward the left clavicle/neck, is associated with a palpable systolic thrill and is augmented by inspiration is the textbook pulmonic area ejection pattern. Inspiration augments right-sided venous return and therefore augments a right heart outflow murmur, and a palpable systolic thrill is a pressure-gradient phenomenon typical of a stenotic orifice rather than a pure high-flow murmur.

The natural history fits chronic fixed outflow obstruction: faint cyanosis in childhood from limited pulmonary blood flow across a stenotic pulmonary valve, later progressive exertional dyspnoea, mild fatigue and light-headedness in adult life, and ECG signs of right ventricular hypertrophy from chronic pressure overload. Left axis deviation can be seen with chronic RV pressure overload with secondary left anterior hemiblock/left anterior fascicular block and does not mandate an AV canal defect.

None of the listed lesions reproduces this pattern:
Ebstein anomaly = holosystolic tricuspid regurgitation at left lower sternal border with RA enlargement;
Patent ductus arteriosus = continuous machinery murmur;
Mitral valve prolapse = mid-late systolic click ± late systolic murmur at apex;
Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with obstruction = dynamic LVOT systolic murmur at left lower sternal border ↑ with Valsalva, ↓ with inspiration;
Tricuspid stenosis = diastolic rumbling ↑ with inspiration;
Ventricular septal defect = harsh holosystolic murmur best at left lower sternal border.

Atrial septal defect, and in particular an ostium primum/AV septal defect, is the closest listed physiologic mimic because increased pulmonary flow can produce an early-mid systolic ejection murmur at the pulmonary area louder with inspiration with RV volume overload and RVH, and an ostium primum defect can give left axis deviation from conduction system disruption. It does not, however, fully account for the palpable systolic thrill and the history of faint childhood cyanosis, both much more specific for true anatomic pulmonic stenosis than for a pure left-to-right atrial flow murmur, and a secundum ASD is classically right axis deviation with a fixed split S2.

Strictly speaking the lesion described is isolated pulmonary stenosis. Pulmonary stenosis is not an option A–G, hence None of these choices. If a forced choice from the list were required, ostium primum ASD, D, is the intended examination mimic.

